---
<Base lang={lang} path="/events/canyon/" title="Canyon Conquest: Lane Strategy" description="The three-lane relay battle where staying power beats power: the relay rules, the 3-fight limit, lane-priority strategy, known bugs, and why this is the game's best zero-risk event.">
  <GuidePage title="Canyon Conquest: Lane Strategy" description="The three-lane relay battle where staying power beats power: the relay rules, the 3-fight limit, lane-priority strategy, known bugs, and why this is the game's best zero-risk event." lang={lang}>
    <p>Canyon Conquest is the <strong>alliance lane battle</strong>: three lanes, and the alliance that wins <strong>2 of 3 lanes</strong> takes the match. But the format is a <strong>relay</strong> — the weakest players fight first, and a player <strong>stays in the lane until they lose</strong>. Understanding what that relay does to lineup strategy is the whole event.</p>
    <p>This guide covers the relay rules, the reward structure, the lane-priority strategy with its main variants, the known bugs, and why this is the safest event in the game.</p>

    <h2 id="relay-rules">The Relay Rules</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Weakest fight first.</strong> Each lane starts with its lowest-CP players and escalates upward only when someone loses.</li>
      <li><strong>The player stays until they lose, then the next enters.</strong> A winning player holds the lane and keeps fighting the next challenger — there is no rotation after a win.</li>
      <li><strong>Max 3 fights per player.</strong> After three fights you are done for the match.</li>
      <li><strong>15 players per lane.</strong> The alliance splits its roster across the three lanes.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Consequence of the relay: <strong>if your side wins every fight at 2 kills each, only 8 of 15 lane players ever fight.</strong> Seven players sit the entire match without spending a fight. That fact drives every lineup decision below.</li>

    <h2 id="rewards">Rewards and the 3-Kill Tier</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The <strong>3-kill reward is tier-gated</strong> — you need all three of your fights to be kills to hit the top personal tier.</li>
      <li>Because stronger players deep in the lane face stronger opponents, the 3-kill tier usually belongs to the top of the roster — plan your personal expectations accordingly.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="rounds-and-groups">Rounds and Groups</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>After every 5 rounds the match ends and restarts from Registration.</strong> Re-register each cycle.</li>
      <li><strong>Win and you move to an upper group; lose and you stay in the same or drop to a weaker group.</strong> The ladder self-sorts alliances over time.</li>
      <li>A <strong>&quot;bye&quot; is possible even at a full 15</strong> — an odd number of registered lanes means someone advances without fighting. It is luck of the draw, not a bug.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="known-bugs">Known Bugs</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Replaced after 2 wins without losing</strong> — the relay should keep you in after wins, but some players get pulled after two victories. Report it if it hits you.</li>
      <li><strong>Registered players excluded from round 2.</strong> Stale registrations can strand you out of the next round. Removal from the list is <strong>R4/R5 only</strong> — if you are stuck, ask leadership to clear you so you can re-register.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="lane-strategy">Lane Priority Strategy</h2>
    <blockquote>You don&apos;t need to win EVERY fight — you need the RIGHT lanes.</blockquote>
    <h3 id="consensus-setup">The Consensus Setup</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Prioritize 2 of the 3 lanes.</strong> Winning the match needs 2 lanes, not 3 — pouring roster evenly into all three just guarantees losing the third anyway.</li>
      <li><strong>Split the top-30 evenly into two strong lanes</strong>, and fill the remaining bodies into the third.</li>
    </ol>
    <h3 id="weak-lane-refinement">The Refinement: Two Strongest to the Weak Lane</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Send your <strong>2 strongest players to the WEAK third lane</strong> — they carry it.</li>
      <li>The logic is relay arithmetic: your strongest players parked in the strong lanes <strong>often never fight</strong>, because the lane&apos;s weaker players win out before the aces are ever needed. In the weak lane, they are the difference between 0-1 and a live lane.</li>
    </ol>
    <h3 id="counterpoint">The Counterpoint That Worked</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>3 balanced teams with the top-3 player in each</strong> has also verifiably worked — an anchor per lane instead of a conceded lane.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Neither setup is wrong in a vacuum. The correct read: <strong>check who won and lost where in the next round.</strong> Your opponents change group by group, and the lane setup that won this match may need rebalancing next round. Adapt, don&apos;t autopilot.</p>

    <h2 id="why-zero-risk">Why This Is the Best Zero-Risk Event</h2>
    <p>Canyon Conquest has <strong>no troop loss, no resource risk, and no real stamina cost</strong> — unlike <a href="/events/kvk/">KvK</a>, where an unshielded city burns. You fight with borrowed tournament power, your real army stays home intact. That makes it one of the <strong>best events for safe rewards</strong> in the game: show up, register, fight your three fights, collect.</p>
    <p>The only real cost is lineup attention — and the relay rules above are the entire lineup meta.</p>

    <VideoSection topic="canyon" lang={lang} />
  </GuidePage>
</Base>
